# Inner Classes

# What is an Inner Class

An inner class is a class declared inside another class.



## Why Use Inner Classes

* Better grouping of related classes
* Improves encapsulation
* Cleaner code structure
* Access outer class members directly


# Types of Inner Classes

Java supports 4 types of inner classes:

1. Member Inner Class
2. Static Nested Class
3. Local Inner Class
4. Anonymous Inner Class



#  1. Member Inner Class

A non-static class declared inside another class.


##  Features

* Can access all outer class members
* Requires outer class object


##  Syntax

class Outer {

    class Inner {

    }
}


## Object Creation

Outer o = new Outer();
Outer.Inner i = o.new Inner();




# 2. Static Nested Class


A static class declared inside another class.


##  Features

* Belongs to outer class
* Does not require outer object
* Can access only static members directly


## Syntax

class Outer {

    static class Inner {

    }
}


##  Object Creation

Outer.Inner obj = new Outer.Inner();


# 3. Local Inner Class

A class declared inside a method.


##  Features

* Accessible only inside that method
* Cannot use access modifiers
* Useful for method-specific logic


## Syntax

void show() {

    class Local {

    }
}




# 4. Anonymous Inner Class

An inner class without a name.

Used for:

* One-time implementation
* Method overriding
* Interfaces/abstract classes



##  Syntax

Animal a = new Animal() {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
};




#  Advantages of Inner Classes

* Better encapsulation
* Cleaner organization
* Improved readability
* Access outer members directly


#  Important Points

| Type                  | Needs Outer Object? | Can be static?  |
| --------------------- | ------------------- | ----------------|
| Member Inner Class    | ✅ Yes              | ❌ No           |
| Static Nested Class   | ❌ No               | ✅ Yes          |
| Local Inner Class     | Depends             | ❌ No           |
| Anonymous Inner Class | Depends             | ❌ No           |



#  Best Practices

* Use member inner class for tightly coupled classes
* Use static nested class if outer object not needed
* Use anonymous class for quick implementation
* Avoid excessive nesting



#  Real-world Usage

* Event handling
* GUI applications
* Callbacks
* Framework internals
* Collections API
